\[
ewcommand{ s}{ oldsymbol} 
ewcommand{\Cbb}{\mathbb{C}} 
ewcommand{\im}{\mathrm{i}\mkern1mu} 
ewcommand{\diag}{\mathrm{diag}}\] <blockquote> <p><strong>Theorem.</strong> A circulant matrix $ s C \in \Cbb^{N	imes N}$ which has been generated using the DFT $ s u =  s{Fv} \in \Cbb^N$ (with $ s F$ the DFT matrix) of a $K$-sparse vector $ s v \in \Cbb^N$ is rank-$K$.</p> </blockquote> <p><strong>Proof.</strong> If $ s v$ is $K$-sparse ( \(\| s v\|_0 \le K\) ), it can be written as</p> <p> egin{equation} \label{eq:sparse}  s v = \sum_{q=0}^{K-1} ho_q  s e_{p_q}. \end{equation}</p> <p>Moreover, let $T( s u)$ be the operator that turns a vector $ s u \in \Cbb^N$ into a circulant matrix, that is, in <em>modulo arithmetic</em> ($u_{k-l} = u_{/k-l/_N}$)</p> \[(T( s u))_{k,l} = u_{k-l}\] <p>We can write $ s C = T( s u) = T( s{Fv})$. We also have $u_a = \sum_{b=0}^{N-1} F_{a,b} v_b$. The circulant matrix at index $jk$ can thus write</p> \[ egin{align} \label{eq:dev}  egin{split} C_{jk} &amp;= u_{j-k} = \sum_{l=0}^{N-1} F_{j-k,l}~ v_l \underset{\eqref{eq:sparse}}{=} \sum_{l=0}^{N-1} F_{j-k,l} \sum_{q=0}^{K-1} ho_q \delta_{l,p_q} \ &amp;= \sum_{q=0}^{K-1} ho_q F_{j-k,p_q } \underbrace{\sum_{l=0}^{N-1} \delta_{l,p_q}}_{=1} \ &amp;= \sum_{q=0}^{K-1} ho_q F_{j,p_q} F_{k,p_q}^* \end{split} \end{align}\] <p>where the last line in \eqref{eq:dev} is obtained thanks to the definition of the coefficients of the DFT matrix $F_{jk} = e^{ rac{-\im 2\pi jk}{N}}$. This is the critical property which allows this proof. Finally, the circulant matrix writes as</p> <p> egin{equation} \label{eq:not1}  s C = \sum_{q=0}^{K-1} ho_q  s f[p_q]  s f[p_q]^* \end{equation}</p> <p>with $ s f[n] :=  s F_{:,n}$, which makes it rank-$K$.</p> <p>The circulant matrix $ s C$ in \eqref{eq:not1} can also be equivalently represented as</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:quasiSVD}  s C =  egin{bmatrix} &amp; &amp; &amp; \ &amp; &amp; &amp; \  s f [p_0] &amp;  s f [p_1] &amp; \dots \ &amp; &amp; &amp; \ &amp; &amp; &amp; \end{bmatrix}  egin{bmatrix} ho_0 &amp; &amp; &amp; \ &amp; ho_1 &amp; &amp; \ &amp; &amp; ho_2 &amp; \ &amp; &amp; &amp; \ddots \end{bmatrix}  egin{bmatrix} &amp; &amp;  s f^*[p_0] &amp; &amp; \ &amp; &amp;  s f^*[p_1] &amp; &amp; \ &amp; &amp; \vdots &amp; &amp; \ \end{bmatrix} =  s{FP} \diag( s ho)  s P^*  s F^*, \end{equation}\] <p>with $ s ho \in \mathbb{R}^K$ containing the $K$ coefficients of the vector $ s v$ which also shows $ s C$ is rank-$K$.</p> <ul>Note:</ul> <p>With the conventional writing $ s v = \sum_{l=0}^{N-1} v_l  s e_l$, a derivation similar to \eqref{eq:dev} would lead to $ s C = \sum_{l=0}^{N-1} v_l  s f[l]  s f[l]^*$.</p> <p>Thus</p> <p> egin{equation} \label{eq:decomp}  s C =  s F \diag(  s v)  s F^* <br/> \end{equation}</p> <p>We see that \eqref{eq:quasiSVD} is very close to the SVD definition: \( s C :=  s{U\Sigma V}^*.\) The subtlety comes where the singular values in $ s\Sigma$ are:</p> <ul> <li><strong>all positive</strong>: this implies \( s C =  s F  s P |\diag( s ho)|  s P^* 	ilde{ s F}^*\) where $	ilde{ s F}^*$ is the Fourier matrix with each column $i$ multiplied by $+1$ or $-1$ depending on the sign of associated $ho_i$.</li> <li><strong>aranged in descending order</strong>: this implies the selection matrix $ s P$ must also contain permutation to ensure $ sho$ is aranged in descending order.</li> </ul> <p>To synthetize, we have</p> \[ egin{align}  egin{split}  s C &amp;=  s F \diag ( s v)  s F^* \ &amp;=  s F \diag(| s v|)  s S  s F^*\ &amp;=  s F  s P^* \diag(|	ilde{ s v}|)  s{PSF}^* \ &amp;=  s{U\Sigma V}^*, \end{split} \end{align}\] <p>with $ s S = \diag(	ext{sign}( s v))$, $ s P$ is a permutation matrix such that \( s P | s v| = |	ilde{ s v}|\) and \(	ilde{v}_k \ge |	ilde{v}_{k+1}|\) , $ s \Sigma= \diag(|	ilde{ s v}|)$, and the unitary matrices \( s U =  s F  s P^*\) , and $ s V =  s F  s S  s P$. By definition, this means that \( s U  s \Sigma  s V^*\) is the SVD decomposition of $ s C$.</p> <p>In fact, \( s{U\Sigma V}^* =  s U  s Z  s \Sigma  s Z^*  s V^*\) for any $ s Z = {m diag}( s z)$ such that $ s Z  s Z^* =  s I$ (that is, $ s z$ is such that $|z_k|=1$). So we can choose $ s z$ to make $ s U s Z$ and $ s V  s Z$ real, as in J. Romberg developments.</p> <p>This proves a rank-$K$ circulant matrix contains only $K$ degrees of freedom, very less than a general rank-$K$ matrix containing $(2K+1)N$ DOFs.</p> <p>We also see a circulant matrix is hermitian if $ s v \in \mathbb{R}^N$ as $ s C^* =  s F \diag( s v)^*  s F^* =  s F \diag( s v)  s F^* =  s C \Leftrightarrow  s v =  s v^*$.</p>

\[
ewcommand{ s}{ oldsymbol} 
ewcommand{\cl}{\mathcal} 
ewcommand{	inv}[1]{ rac{1}{#1}} 
ewcommand{\Cbb}{\mathbb{C}} 
ewcommand{\Rbb}{\mathbb{R}} 
ewcommand{\im}{\mathrm{i}\mkern1mu} 
ewcommand{\diag}{\mathrm{diag}} 
ewcommand{\upto}[1]{\llbracket #1 rbracket}\] <p>In the aim to compute an approximate solution to the linear inverse problem $ s{Ax}= s y$ with $ s A \in \Rbb^{n	imes n}$ of rank $r \leq n$, one is interested in evaluating the loss \(\cl L ( s x^{(k)},  s y) = 	inv 2 \| s A  s x^{(k)} - s y\|_2^2\) when $ s x^{(k)}$ is the $k$-th iterate of the <em>Gradient Descent</em> algorithm with zero initialization. It writes $ s x^{(k)} = \sum_{i=0}^k ( s I-\alpha  s A^*  s A)^i \alpha  s A^*  s y$, and writing the SVD decomposition \( s A :=  s U  s \Sigma  s V^*\) with the properties $ s V^*  s V =  s I_r$ and $ s U^*  s U =  s I_r$, one gets</p> \[ egin{align*}  s A  s x^{(k)} &amp;= \sum_{i=0}^k  s U  s \Sigma  s V^* ( s I_n - \alpha  s V  s\Sigma^2  s V^*) ( s I_n - \alpha  s A^*  s A)^{i-1} \alpha  s A^*  s y \ &amp;= \sum_{i=0}^k  s U  s \Sigma ( s I_r - \alpha  s\Sigma^2)  s V^* ( s I_n - \alpha  s A^*  s A)^{i-1} \alpha  s A^*  s y \ &amp;= \sum_{i=0}^k  s U  s \Sigma ( s I_r - \alpha  s\Sigma^2)^i  s V^* \alpha  s A^*  s y \ &amp;=  s U  s\Sigma \underbrace{\sum_{i=0}^k ( s I_r - \alpha  s\Sigma^2)^i}_{(\alpha  s\Sigma^2)^{-1} ( s I_r - ( s I_r - \alpha  s\Sigma^2)^{k+1})} \alpha  s\Sigma  s U^*  s y \ &amp;=  s U  s\Sigma^{-1} ( s I_r - ( s I_r - \alpha  s\Sigma^2)^{k+1})  s\Sigma  s U^*  s y \ &amp;=  s U ( s I_r - ( s I_r - \alpha  s\Sigma^2)^{k+1})  s U^*  s y. \end{align*}\] <p>Hence,</p> \[ egin{align} \label{eq:loss}  egin{split} \cl L ( s x^{(k)},  s y) &amp;= 	inv 2  igg\|\underbrace{( s U  s U^* -  s I_n)  s y}_{\perp 	ext{Im }  s U } -  s U \diag  igg( 1-\eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{\mathrm{max}}}  ig)^2  igg)^{k+1}  s U^*  s y  igg\|_2^2 \ &amp;= 	inv 2  igg\| ( s U  s U^* -  s I_n)  s y  igg\|_2^2 + 	inv 2  igg\|  s U \diag  igg( 1-\eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2  igg)^{k+1}  s U^*  s y  igg\|_2^2 \ &amp;= 	inv 2  igg\| ( s U  s U^* -  s I_n)  s y  igg\|_2^2 + 	inv 2  igg\| \diag  igg( 1-\eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2  igg)^{k+1}  s U^*  s y  igg\|_2^2 \ &amp;\leq 	inv 2  igg\| ( s U  s U^* -  s I_n)  s y  igg\|_2^2 + 	inv 2 \max_{i \in \upto{r}}  igg| 1- \eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2  igg|^{2(k+1)}  ig\|  s U^*  s y  ig\|_2^2. \end{split} \end{align}\] <p>with \(\alpha 	riangleq  rac{\eta}{\| s A\|^2}.\) The third line is obtained from the second line by using the identity</p> \[\| s{UM} \|_2^2=\langle  s U  s M,  s U  s M angle = \langle  s U^*  s U  s M,  s M angle = \langle  s M,  s M angle=\| s M \|_2^2.\] <p>By convention $\sigma_{\mathrm max} = \sigma_1$. In \eqref{eq:loss}, the first error term is independent of $k$. One cannot do better than targetting the fastest convergence to zero of the second term. For a fixed stepsize $\eta$, the best choice must satisfy</p> \[ egin{align*} \min_\eta \max_{i \in \upto{r} }  igg| 1- \eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2  igg| &amp;= \min_\eta \max  igg( \max_{i \in \upto{r} } 1- \eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2, \max_{i \in \upto{r} } \eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2 -1  igg) \ &amp;= \min_\eta \max  igg( 1- \eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_r}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2,\eta -1  igg) \end{align*}\] <p>which is reached when</p> \[ egin{align} \label{eq:eta}  egin{split} &amp;1 - \eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_r}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2 = \eta - 1 \ \Leftrightarrow ~&amp; \eta =  rac{2}{1+(\sigma_r/\sigma_1)^2}. \end{split} \end{align}\] <p>In particular, injecting \eqref{eq:eta} into \eqref{eq:loss} yields</p> \[ egin{align*} \cl L_k ( s x^{(k)},  s y) &amp;\leq 	inv 2  igg\| ( s U  s U^* -  s I_n)  s y  igg\|_2^2 + 	inv 2  ig| \eta - 1  ig|^{2(k+1)}  ig\|  s U^*  s y  ig\|_2^2 \ &amp;= 	inv 2  igg\| ( s U  s U^* -  s I_n)  s y  igg\|_2^2 + 	inv 2  igg|  rac{1-(\sigma_r/\sigma_1)^2 }{1+(\sigma_r/\sigma_1)^2}  igg|^{2(k+1)}  ig\|  s U^*  s y  ig\|_2^2, \end{align*}\] <p>meaning that the decreasing of the loss with the iterations of the gradient descent is slower when $\sigma_r \ll \sigma_1$.</p> <center> <img src="/assets/img/ideal_stepsize.png" alt="Gradient Descent iterations" width="500px"/> </center> <p><strong>Figure 1</strong>: Gradient Descent iterations as a function of the stepsize for the system $ s A  s x =  s y$ with $ s A \in \Rbb^{30	imes 30}$ of rank $r=5$.<br/> In this experiment, the ideal choice following \eqref{eq:eta} was $\eta^\star = 1.26$.</p>
